### Bug Description

When a Code node calls `console.log()` with a `null` / `undefined` argument during a **manual** execution, n8n throws and logs `There was a problem sending message to UI: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'isLuxonDateTime')` for every single call, instead of forwarding the value to the editor console.

Root cause is in `sendMessageToUI()` (`n8n-core`, `execution-engine/node-execution-context/base-execute-context.js`):

```js
args = args.map((arg) => {
    if (arg.isLuxonDateTime && arg.invalidReason)   // throws when arg is null/undefined
        return { ...arg };
    if (arg.isLuxonDateTime)
        return new Date(arg.ts).toString();
    if (arg instanceof Date)
        return arg.toString();
    return arg;
});
```

`arg.isLuxonDateTime` on a `null`/`undefined` argument throws a TypeError. `console.log(null)` is perfectly valid JavaScript and should be handled gracefully.

In our deployment this produced a **sustained error flood**: a Code node filters a large dataset and logs one line per skipped item, where some fields are nullable, e.g.:

```js
if (!hasValue) {
    console.log("skipping row", item.json.field); // field can be null
}
```

We observed ~77,000 occurrences of `There was a problem sending message to UI: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'isLuxonDateTime')` within ~3 hours (peak ~30 errors/second). In the same window the n8n process V8 heap grew until it hit the heap limit and crashed with `FATAL ERROR: Reached heap limit Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory`. The error flood (per-error object/stack-trace allocation) appears to be a significant contributor to heap growth, though we have not proven causation.

Important detail: the crash only happens in **manual** mode — `sendMessageToUI()` early-returns when `this.mode !== 'manual'` — so production/scheduled executions are unaffected and the bug is easy to miss until someone runs the workflow from the editor.

### To Reproduce

1. Self-hosted n8n (Docker), observed on `2.34.5` (code path unchanged since at least 2.30.x)
2. Create a Code node with `console.log(null); return [{ json: {} }];`
3. Execute the workflow **manually** from the editor
4. Observe in n8n logs: `There was a problem sending message to UI: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'isLuxonDateTime')`

### Expected Behavior

- `console.log(null)` / `console.log(undefined)` should not throw; the message should reach the editor console (e.g. rendered as `null` / `undefined`)
- No error spam in server logs for valid Code node code

### Proposed Fix

Guard against `null`/`undefined` in the args normalization inside `sendMessageToUI()`:

```js
args = args.map((arg) => {
    if (arg === null || arg === undefined) return String(arg);
    if (arg.isLuxonDateTime && arg.invalidReason) return { ...arg };
    if (arg.isLuxonDateTime) return new Date(arg.ts).toString();
    if (arg instanceof Date) return arg.toString();
    return arg;
});
```

A regression test should cover `console.log(null)`, `console.log(undefined)` and `console.log("x", null)` in a manually executed Code node.

### Debug Info

- Deployment: Self-hosted Docker (docker compose)
- First affected version observed: 2.34.5 (also verified present in earlier 2.x by source inspection)
- Node.js: 24.18.0
- Database: PostgreSQL 18
- Execution mode: regular (main process), task runners external
- Operating system: Ubuntu 22.04
- Related: #15111 (task runner logging to browser console)

## Bug Description

Static data written from a webhook trigger's `webhook()` is not persisted on n8n 2.19.2 and later. The write is visible during the execution, but the next delivery starts from the previously stored state, so counters, cursors and de-duplication records kept in node static data silently stop working. 1.x and 2.19.0 persist it.

2.19.2 is the first affected release. It carries the backport of #28973 ("Persist execution context before writing to db"). `WorkflowRunner.establishContextForPersistence` builds a `Workflow` over `data.workflowData.staticData`, the same object the webhook has just modified. `ObservableObject.create` defines `__dataChanged = false` on that top-level object, so the change flag is cleared before the static data is saved.

## To Reproduce

1. Save the node below as `StaticDataRepro.node.js` in a directory listed in `N8N_CUSTOM_EXTENSIONS` and start n8n.
2. Create a workflow with the `Static Data Repro` trigger followed by any node, and publish it.
3. POST to its production webhook URL three times, one request after another.
4. Compare the output of the three executions and the workflow's static data (`GET /rest/workflows/:id`, or the `staticData` column of `workflow_entity`).

<details>
<summary>StaticDataRepro.node.js</summary>

```js
// Minimal webhook trigger: registers a remote id on activation (as real triggers do) and counts
// deliveries in node static data from inside webhook().
class StaticDataRepro {
  constructor() {
    this.description = {
      displayName: 'Static Data Repro',
      name: 'staticDataRepro',
      group: ['trigger'],
      version: 1,
      description: 'Counts deliveries in node static data',
      defaults: { name: 'Static Data Repro' },
      inputs: [],
      outputs: ['main'],
      webhooks: [{ name: 'default', httpMethod: 'POST', responseMode: 'onReceived', path: 'repro' }],
      properties: [],
    };
    this.webhookMethods = {
      default: {
        async checkExists() {
          return this.getWorkflowStaticData('node').remoteId !== undefined;
        },
        async create() {
          this.getWorkflowStaticData('node').remoteId = 'remote-1';
          return true;
        },
        async delete() {
          delete this.getWorkflowStaticData('node').remoteId;
          return true;
        },
      },
    };
  }

  async webhook() {
    const data = this.getWorkflowStaticData('node');
    data.count = (data.count ?? 0) + 1;
    return { workflowData: [this.helpers.returnJsonArray({ count: data.count })] };
  }
}

module.exports = { StaticDataRepro };
```

</details>

| n8n | `count` in executions 1, 2, 3 | persisted static data |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1.123.87 | 1, 2, 3 | `{"remoteId":"remote-1","count":3}` |
| 2.19.0 | 1, 2, 3 | `{"remoteId":"remote-1","count":3}` |
| 2.19.2 | 1, 1, 1 | `{"remoteId":"remote-1"}` |
| 2.45.3 | 1, 1, 1 | `{"remoteId":"remote-1"}` |

The node registers a remote ID in `webhookMethods.create`, as real triggers do, so the stored static data is not empty. With an empty store the first delivery is persisted by accident, because the extra `Workflow` is then built over a separate object, and every later delivery is lost. A community trigger that keeps a de-duplication record in static data behaves the same on 2.20.0, 2.30.0, 2.41.6, 2.44.4 and 2.44.6.

## Expected behavior

Each execution reports the incremented count and the workflow keeps `count` in its static data, as on 1.x and 2.19.0.

## Cause and fix

`establishContextForPersistence` only needs the workflow settings and node lookups, and its comment says "no staticData loading", yet it passes `staticData`. Dropping that argument restores `1, 2, 3` and persists `count` on 2.45.3 (checked by patching the compiled `workflow-runner.js`). `master` has the same code today.

This is not #39653, which is about reloading static data on queue workers.

I will open a PR with a regression test.

## Debug Info

