  platform: npm
  nodeJsVersion: 24.22.1
  database: sqlite
  executionMode: regular
```

- Operating System: macOS 27.0.1
- n8n Version: 2.45.3 (first affected: 2.19.2)
- Node.js Version: 24.22.1
- Database: SQLite
- Execution mode: main

### Bug Description

Follow-up to #42394 and #42436. The patch bounds long non-whitespace runs, but long runs of mixed whitespace can still block the main event loop.

With the patched counter, an 8,000-character string of alternating spaces and tabs blocked the event loop for **12.1 seconds**. Ordinary text of the same length took about **13 ms**. No model, credentials, network request or customer data is needed to reproduce this.

We encountered this after a native Agent produced an unfinished JSON tool call. The resulting parse error contained a 388,313-character run of spaces, tabs and newlines. The main process then used roughly one CPU core and `/healthz/readiness` timed out repeatedly. A cancellation request did not complete before we restarted the main container. Docker reported no OOM kill; workers remained running. That incident is supporting context, not a captured production CPU trace.

### Root cause

There are two connected gaps:

1. **Whitespace bypasses the short-run safeguard.** [`findLongRunStart`](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/cdc3f4c253b6069cf1087eabc88d1b0144a9b8da/packages/%40n8n/agents/src/runtime/model/model-token-counter.ts#L40-L70) resets its run start at every whitespace character. The 200-character bound therefore never applies to an all-whitespace input. An entire 8,000-character window can reach synchronous `encoder.encode()`. The time-based yield is checked only after that call returns.
2. **An already-rejected SDK tool result can reach the budget counter without the normal error guard.** [`fromAiMessages`](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/cdc3f4c253b6069cf1087eabc88d1b0144a9b8da/packages/%40n8n/agents/src/runtime/model/messages.ts#L570-L577) retains an `error-text` result as a rejected call. [`processToolCall`](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/cdc3f4c253b6069cf1087eabc88d1b0144a9b8da/packages/%40n8n/agents/src/runtime/tools/tool-call-runner.ts#L101-L114) takes the already-settled branch before the ordinary error handler. [`serializeMessageForBudget`](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/cdc3f4c253b6069cf1087eabc88d1b0144a9b8da/packages/%40n8n/agents/src/runtime/memory/memory-orchestrator.ts#L73-L85) includes the rejected error verbatim, and [`estimateVisibleBudget`](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/cdc3f4c253b6069cf1087eabc88d1b0144a9b8da/packages/%40n8n/agents/src/runtime/memory/memory-orchestrator.ts#L626-L635) passes it to the counter.

The second point is an error-handling gap, not a tool-permission bypass: the invalid tool never executes.

### To Reproduce

Run this in a **disposable process with an external timeout**, using the Agent package built with #42436. Do not run it inside the live n8n process. The path below is the standard global installation path in the n8n Docker image; adjust it for a source build.

```js
const { createRequire } = require('node:module');
const { setTimeout: delay } = require('node:timers/promises');
const req = createRequire(
  '/usr/local/lib/node_modules/n8n/node_modules/@n8n/agents/package.json',
);
const { createModelTokenCounter } = req(
  './dist/runtime/model/model-token-counter.js',
);

(async () => {
  // This selects o200k_base. It does not call a model.
  const counter = createModelTokenCounter('openai/gpt-5');
  await counter('warm up');

  let last = performance.now();
  let maxGapMs = 0;
  const heartbeat = setInterval(() => {
    const now = performance.now();
    maxGapMs = Math.max(maxGapMs, now - last);
    last = now;
  }, 1);
  const start = performance.now();
  try {
    const tokens = await counter(' \t'.repeat(4000));
    await delay(0);
    console.log({ tokens, totalMs: performance.now() - start, maxGapMs });
  } finally {
    clearInterval(heartbeat);
  }
})().catch(error => { console.error(error); process.exitCode = 1; });
```

Fresh measurements from separate containers, each limited to one CPU and 768 MiB, with networking disabled and a 35-second external timeout:

| Input / counter | Total time | Longest event-loop pause | Tokens |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8,000 characters of ordinary prose, upstream counter | 12.9 ms | 11.5 ms | 1,281 |
| 8,000 alternating spaces/tabs, upstream counter | 12,101.8 ms | 12,101.8 ms | 3,999 |
| Same spaces/tabs, temporary 200-character window | 327.3 ms | 37.3 ms | 3,979 |

The prose fixture was `'ordinary synthetic text. '.repeat(334).slice(0, 8000)`.

The last row changes only `TOKEN_COUNT_CHUNK_CHARS` from 8,000 to 200 in a temporary in-process copy. It is a causal control, **not a deployed fix**. It also changes the estimate slightly, so it is not a claim that globally shrinking the window is the correct final implementation.

A second run with CPU profiling took 11.6 seconds. Of 10,062 samples, 9,556 (94.97%) were directly in:

```text
countInChunks
  -> encoder.encode
     -> bytePairEncode
        -> bytePairMerge  (js-tiktoken/dist/lite.cjs)
```

### Rejected-error path check

A separate functional check used the real `fromAiMessages`, `ToolCallRunner` and `MemoryOrchestrator`, with injected counters that recorded text and returned `1`. It made no tokenizer or model calls.

The synthetic input was `"Synthetic parser error: " + " \t".repeat(32000)`, supplied as an SDK `error-text` tool result. Assertions passed:

```json
{
  "alreadySettledGuardCounterCalls": 0,
  "ordinaryErrorGuardCounterCalls": 1,
  "toolExecutions": 0,
  "rejectedBudgetLiteralTabs": 32000,
  "resolvedBudgetLiteralTabs": 0,
  "rejectedBudgetRawErrorPreserved": true,
  "cachedMessageNotRecounted": true
}
```

The ordinary-error control invoked the error guard. The resolved-result control serialized its tabs as escapes. The rejected-result budget path retained the literal tabs. This establishes a route for malformed tool-call output to reach the slow counter; it does not establish why the model generated the malformed arguments.

<details>
<summary>Complete synthetic rejected-error path check</summary>

Run in the same disposable environment as the counter reproduction.

```js
// Synthetic functional check only: no model, network, filesystem writes or real tokenizer.
// Run in a disposable n8n process. The injected counters record text, then return 1.
const assert = require('node:assert/strict');
const { createRequire } = require('node:module');
const root = '/usr/local/lib/node_modules/n8n/node_modules/@n8n/agents';
const req = createRequire(root + '/package.json');
const { fromAiMessages } = req('./dist/runtime/model/messages.js');
const { ToolCallRunner } = req('./dist/runtime/tools/tool-call-runner.js');
const { MemoryOrchestrator } = req('./dist/runtime/memory/memory-orchestrator.js');

const error = 'Synthetic parser error: ' + ' \t'.repeat(32000);
const toolName = 'synthetic_tool';
const toolCallId = 'synthetic_call';
const messages = fromAiMessages([
  { role: 'assistant', content: [{ type: 'tool-call', toolCallId, toolName, input: {} }] },
  { role: 'tool', content: [{ type: 'tool-result', toolCallId, toolName,
    output: { type: 'error-text', value: error } }] },
]);
assert.equal(messages.length, 1);
const block = messages[0].content.find(part => part.type === 'tool-call');
assert.equal(block.state, 'rejected');
assert.equal(block.error, error);

(async () => {
  const events = [], guardCounterInputs = [], recordedErrors = [];
  const eventBus = { emit: event => events.push(event) };
  let executed = 0;
  const tool = { name: toolName, execute: () => { executed++; throw Error('Must not execute'); } };
  const runner = new ToolCallRunner({ eventBus,
    tokenCounter: async text => { guardCounterInputs.push(text); return 1; } });
  const params = { toolName, toolCallId, input: {}, toolMap: new Map([[toolName, tool]]),
    list: { responseDelta: () => messages,
      setToolCallError: (id, value) => recordedErrors.push({id, value}) } };
  const settled = await runner.processToolCall(params);
  assert.equal(settled.outcome, 'noop');
  assert.equal(executed, 0);
  assert.equal(guardCounterInputs.length, 0);
  assert.equal(recordedErrors.length, 0);
  assert.equal(events.length, 1);
  assert.equal(events[0].isError, true);
  assert.equal(events[0].result, error);

  // Positive control: the ordinary error handler does invoke the real guard.
  // It is called directly to isolate error handling from execution/telemetry setup.
  assert.equal(typeof runner.toolError, 'function');
  const ordinary = await runner.toolError(params, error, tool);
  assert.equal(ordinary.outcome, 'error');
  assert.equal(guardCounterInputs.length, 1);
  assert.equal(guardCounterInputs[0].includes('\t'), false);
  assert.equal(guardCounterInputs[0].includes('\\t'), true);
  assert.equal(recordedErrors.length, 1);

  const captured = [];
  const memory = new MemoryOrchestrator({}, {}, eventBus, {}, async text => {
    captured.push(text); return 1;
  });
  const message = { ...messages[0], id: 'synthetic_message' };
  const list = { llmVisibleMessages: () => [message] };
  assert.equal(typeof memory.estimateVisibleBudget, 'function');
  assert.equal(await memory.estimateVisibleBudget(list), 1);
  assert.equal(captured.length, 1);
  assert.equal(captured[0].endsWith(error), true);
  assert.equal((captured[0].match(/\t/g) || []).length, 32000);
  assert.equal(await memory.estimateVisibleBudget(list), 1);
  assert.equal(captured.length, 1); // Existing per-message cache still works.

  // Resolved strings use JSON serialization; rejected error-text remains raw.
  const resolved = { ...message, id: 'synthetic_resolved', content: [
    { type: 'tool-call', toolCallId, toolName, input: {}, state: 'resolved', output: error },
  ] };
  await memory.estimateVisibleBudget({ llmVisibleMessages: () => [resolved] });
  assert.equal(captured.length, 2);
  assert.equal(captured[1].includes('\t'), false);
  assert.equal(captured[1].includes('\\t'), true);

  console.log(JSON.stringify({ passed: true, syntheticErrorChars: error.length,
    alreadySettledGuardCounterCalls: 0, ordinaryErrorGuardCounterCalls: guardCounterInputs.length,
    toolExecutions: executed, rejectedBudgetLiteralTabs: 32000,
    resolvedBudgetLiteralTabs: 0, rejectedBudgetRawErrorPreserved: true,
    cachedMessageNotRecounted: true, modelCalls: 0, realTokenizerCalls: 0 }));
})().catch(error => { console.error(error.stack); process.exitCode = 1; });
```

</details>

### Expected behavior

Token counting should bound synchronous work for whitespace-heavy input as well as long words. A rejected tool call should not leave the main process unresponsive while its error is counted. The already-settled error path needs the same size/work protections before memory-budget estimation.

A regression test should measure the maximum event-loop pause for mixed whitespace, not only whether the counter eventually yields. The rejected SDK error-text path also needs coverage.

### Debug Info / Environment

- n8n **2.45.5 with the exact upstream #42436 backport**, not an unmodified release.
- Counter source: merge commit `cdc3f4c253b6069cf1087eabc88d1b0144a9b8da`. Current master had the same counter file when checked on 29 October 2026 (blob `84bcd710ec72814d1a9b56036368dbe39ca45944`).
- Node.js **v26.12.1**, Linux x64, Docker, self-hosted.
- `js-tiktoken` **1.0.21**, `o200k_base`.
- PostgreSQL, queue mode. The synthetic reproduction does not access the database or Redis.
- No private prompts, ticket contents, credentials, hostnames, session IDs or raw production logs are included.

## Bug description

The native Agent's mid-run memory observer can be told that a stored tool result has expired **while the originating run is still active and can successfully read that result**.

The result is not actually missing. `sanitizeOffloadedToolResultsForMemory()` intentionally removes run-scoped file references before persistence, replacing them with:

```json
{"_offloaded":true,"expired":true,"message":"The stored tool result expired with its originating run."}
```

`persistTurnDelta()` applies this sanitisation during a live run. `runObservationLogObserver()` then builds its transcript from the persisted messages, not the live message list. It therefore receives an expiry assertion that is only appropriate for later replay. An accepted observation can preserve this false assertion after the covered original messages are masked.

## Observed evidence (sanitised)

In one existing run:

1. An MCP search result was offloaded to a run-scoped workspace file (approximately 245 KB). The live tool-call trace retained the normal `_offloaded` envelope and read instructions.
2. The persisted version of that same tool call contained only the expired-result marker.
3. A mid-run factual observation consequently recorded the search result as expired/unavailable for corroboration (paraphrased; private text omitted).
4. The same run subsequently read the file successfully through `workspace_read_tool_result`. There was no intervening new search result or recreated file.

The unrelated attachment-summary omission in that investigation is **not** the subject of this report. This report concerns the deterministic premature expiry marker. No claim is made that fixing this alone prevents all repeated tool calls.

No customer data, private prompts, tool payloads, actual file paths, execution IDs, hostnames, credentials, screenshots or absolute operational timestamps are included here. The reproducer below uses generated synthetic paths only and calls no AI provider.

## Expected behaviour

During a live run, the observer should see a current result reference as current, using the same model-facing trust and size guards. Later replay must still receive the expired marker, and results must remain run/principal scoped. Do not solve this by globally retaining sandbox references in persisted history or disabling result cleanup.

## Source analysis

- [Persistence and observer scheduling](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/master/packages/@n8n/agents/src/runtime/memory/memory-orchestrator.ts): `persistTurnDelta()` sanitises its messages; both hard- and soft-threshold observation paths persist before scheduling the observer.
- [Memory sanitiser](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/master/packages/@n8n/agents/src/runtime/memory/tool-result-memory.ts): direct envelopes, text/serialized envelopes and content-shaped tool results become `EXPIRED_OFFLOADED_TOOL_RESULT`.
- [Observer](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/master/packages/@n8n/agents/src/runtime/memory/observation-log-observer.ts): obtains its delta from `getMessagesForObservationScope()` and renders that persisted representation.
- [Visible message masking](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/master/packages/@n8n/agents/src/runtime/model/message-list.ts): accepted observation cursors hide covered messages from the model window.

The same relevant code is present in the current default branch inspected for this report. File blob SHAs: `memory-orchestrator.ts` c993f32e3167158673f276c17758d5cd604ac102; `observation-log-observer.ts` 24682663ecff0e5c32264c356c56d3886b860315; `tool-result-memory.ts` d99e2072fb07c5a335db33465aaac41531b8ae9e.

Related work: #36203 deliberately sanitised persisted memory when making results run-scoped. #42909 improves JSON Pointer guidance; it does not address the persisted/live observer mismatch. Focused issue/PR searches found no existing report specifically covering this mismatch.

## Proposed fix

Pass an immutable snapshot of **this run's** message delta to mid-run observer tasks. Once the observer has fetched its normal scoped/keyset-filtered database delta, overlay content only for matching message IDs from the snapshot. Keep the database message order, IDs and timestamps for cursor commits. Apply the existing pending-tool barrier and observer redaction/trust/size handling to the overlaid messages.

The patch does not change persistence sanitisation, replay loading, filesystem permissions, cleanup, result-path validation or post-run observer scheduling. Historical messages without a matching current-run entry stay expired. The snapshot is captured before queueing so a subsequent list mutation cannot change the queued observer's input. No public/model-facing parameter is added.

This is a proposed upstream patch, **not deployed**. It addresses a false expiry claim, not guaranteed semantic retention of every fact by the observer model. References remain ephemeral; observations should not treat a run-scoped pointer as a permanent source.

